lone would give. The 1890s hold the most record highs of any decade (47). Wilmington's all-time high is 107\u00b0F, on August 7, 1918.","t_chg":"Wilmington has warmed, days and nights alike","p_chg":"Across 108 complete years: Wilmington's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.28\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.21 to +0.35), about +3.0\u00b0F since 1917. Wilmington's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.20\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.12 to +0.28), about +2.2\u00b0F since 1917.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 9 of 12 months. Highs rose in 5 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Wilmington's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.25 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 45.2 inches a year. The wettest year was 2018 (61.37 in); the driest, 1965 (24.90 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 13 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}